{
  "teamId": 145,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Chicago White Sox arrived at the season carrying the weight of recent lean years, and their March stumble — a 1-4 start punctuated by Christian Yelich's devastating three-run home run off Seranthony Domínguez on Opening Weekend — suggested more of the same. But the roster had genuine talent, and the team refused to stay down. April steadied at .500, built on clutch moments like Derek Hill's bunt single and Tristan Peters' game-winning follow-up, while Andrew Benintendi's three-run bomb late in the month signaled that this offense had teeth.\n\nMay was when Chicago truly announced itself. An 18-10 month — the best of the season — was fueled by a power surge that felt almost electric. Tristan Peters and Edgar Quero hit back-to-back home runs in a May 17th statement game, Munetaka Murakami was doing Murakami things deep into right field, and Miguel Vargas capped the month with a walk-off two-run shot that carried a jaw-dropping 0.852 WPA. The South Side faithful had something to believe in. June continued that momentum with Sam Antonacci's clutch walk-off single and Randal Grichuk's solo shot, and despite a handful of bullpen misfires from Domínguez, the Sox stayed afloat at 13-12.\n\nJuly and August were the grind — a combined 27-26 stretch that tested the team's resolve. Grant Taylor surrendered a devastating two-run homer early in July, and the bullpen leaked in August against Boston and Chicago rivals alike. But the offense kept punching back: Chase Meidroth went deep, Tristan Peters hit another, and Andrew Benintendi continued his quietly excellent season. Randal Grichuk's massive home run on September 12th, a 0.725 WPA swing, briefly re-energized a September fade, and the Sox ultimately ground out an 84-78 record — enough for the postseason.\n\nThe ALDS brought the Cleveland Guardians, and with them, Jo Adell doing inexplicable damage against a team not supposed to produce him. Murakami homered in Game 1 and Braden Montgomery delivered a clutch Game 2 double, but Cleveland's bats — led by José Ramírez, who homered twice in the series — proved too much. Adell's triples in Games 2 and 3 broke rallies and backs alike, and closer Jordan Hicks gave up a crushing Ramírez two-run shot in Game 4 to seal Chicago's fate. The White Sox had made October for the first time in years. They just couldn't quite conquer it.",
